--TEST--
a contagious parameter still refuses a tainted argument when its answer is an instance or a nullable instance, and admits one when its answer is a `mixed` or an `array<mixed>`, whose text is tainted on the way out — `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`, `rule:security/taint-propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_types::expr::quals::admits_tainted_argument`'s doc comment owns why:
// admitting an argument the answer cannot carry the qualifier out of would
// launder it, so the refusal stays until there is somewhere to put the bit.
// Every member below is `Qual::Contagious` in the parameter that refuses --
// the classification is not what stops these, the return type is.
tainted string $t = "a";
tainted bytes $tb = "ab" as bytes;

// An instance answer: `Core\Uri` has no qualifier to carry.
Core\Uri $uri = Core\Uri::parse($t);

// A nullable instance: a union whose members are an instance and `null`, so
// neither member can carry it either.
?Core\Regex\Match $matched = Core\Regex::match($t, "a");

// A `mixed` and an `array<mixed>` answer compile: text read out of `mixed`
// is `tainted`, so the mark has somewhere to go.
mixed $decoded = Core\Json::decode($t);
array<mixed> $fields = Core\Bytes::unpack($tb, "C2");
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:11:33
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:15:48
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
